From: Holodor <holodor@bonesaw.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 23:40:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] steamcompmgr: sanitize GAMESCOPE_COLOR_NIGHT_MODE values

Steam writes three floats (amount, hue, saturation) into the property. On an AYN Odin 3
running Holodor (2026-09-27) it wrote amount 0, hue 0.95, saturation NaN. color_helpers
computes clamp01(saturation * amount) for the night-mode tint, and NaN * 0 is NaN, so the
tint is applied with a NaN saturation whatever the amount says: the whole output turned a
uniform red and no Steam display toggle could clear it, because every update carried the
same NaN. Treat non-finite values as 0 and clamp amount/saturation to [0, 1]; a NaN can
then only ever mean "identity".

--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -45,6 +45,7 @@
 #include <atomic>
 #include <vector>
 #include <algorithm>
+#include <cmath>
 #include <array>
 #include <iostream>
 #include <fstream>
@@ -6505,7 +6506,14 @@
 		if ( bHasVec && user_vec.size() == 3 )
 		{
 			for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
+			{
 				vec[i] = bit_cast<float>( user_vec[i] );
+				// Holodor: Steam has been seen sending NaN here (saturation); NaN survives
+				// clamp01(saturation * amount) and paints the whole output. Non-finite -> 0.
+				if ( !std::isfinite( vec[i] ) )
+					vec[i] = 0.0f;
+			}
+			vec[0] = std::clamp( vec[0], 0.0f, 1.0f ); vec[2] = std::clamp( vec[2], 0.0f, 1.0f );
 		}
 
 		nightmode_t nightmode;
